\title{mzspeclib: Read and Write HUPO-PSI mzSpecLib Spectral Libraries}
\description{
mzSpecLib is the HUPO-PSI exchange format for spectral libraries. A library
file describes a spectral library with controlled vocabulary (PSI-MS)
parameters on four levels - the library itself, its spectra, the analytes
and interpretations of a spectrum - plus, for every spectrum, a peak list.
Version 1.0 of the specification defines two normative serializations of
that model, a line oriented plain text format and a JSON format, and this
package reads and writes both.
}
\section{Getting started}{

\code{\link[=read_mzspeclib]{read_mzspeclib()}} reads a file and returns a \code{\link[=mzspeclib]{mzspeclib()}} object;
\code{\link[=write_mzspeclib]{write_mzspeclib()}} writes such an object again. Everything else in the
package are accessors for the object: \code{\link[=libraryCv]{libraryCv()}} and \code{\link[=libraryTable]{libraryTable()}} for
the library header, \code{\link[=spectraCv]{spectraCv()}} and \code{\link[=spectraTable]{spectraTable()}} for the spectra,
\code{\link[=spectraPeaks]{spectraPeaks()}} for the peak lists as data.frames,
\code{\link[=analyteCv]{analyteCv()}}/\code{\link[=analyteTable]{analyteTable()}}, \code{\link[=interpretationCv]{interpretationCv()}}/\code{\link[=interpretationTable]{interpretationTable()}},
\code{\link[=clusterCv]{clusterCv()}}/\code{\link[=clusterTable]{clusterTable()}}, \code{\link[=attributeSets]{attributeSets()}} for the attribute set
definitions, \code{\link[=cvParameter]{cvParameter()}} to pick single parameters out of a CV table, and
\code{\link[=peakColumns]{peakColumns()}} for the meaning of the additional peak columns.
}

\section{Two views on the metadata}{

Every level of the model is offered twice, because the two forms are needed
by different consumers.

The \verb{*Cv()} accessors return the parameters \emph{as written}, one row per
parameter, with nothing merged, reordered or dropped. That is the faithful
view, and the one to use when a file has to be understood or reproduced.

The \verb{*Table()} accessors return one row per entity - one row per spectrum -
and one column per parameter, with repeated parameters collapsed into one
cell. That is the form a data.frame consumer wants: \code{\link[=spectraTable]{spectraTable()}} gives a
\code{data.frame} with a column per spectrum attribute, ready to be joined with
the peak matrices of \code{\link[=spectraPeaks]{spectraPeaks()}}. \verb{*Table()} accessors inherit the
attribute sets a spectrum claims (see \code{\link[=attributeSets]{attributeSets()}}), which the \verb{*Cv()}
accessors do not.
}

\section{Design decisions}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Plain lists and data.frames, with S3 classes only for printing and
subsetting.} The format is a tree of sections; a class hierarchy would
add nothing to reading or writing it, and a plain object can be inspected
with \code{str()} and passed to a downstream package without that package
having to import a class definition.
\item \strong{No ontology lookups, and no type conversion based on the ontology.}
Nothing in this package asks a PSI-MS copy what a term means. Values are
kept exactly as the file wrote them, as \code{character}, and only peak \code{mz}
and \code{intensity} are converted to \code{double}. That keeps a result - and a
publication that quotes one - independent of which ontology version
happens to be installed. The \code{coerce} argument of the \verb{*Table()} accessors
offers type guessing from the \emph{values} alone, on request.
\item \strong{Nothing is dropped because the package does not know a term.} An
unknown accession, an unknown peak attribute and even an unknown section
type are read into the object; deviations that do cost something (a
section without a key, a JSON member the specification does not define)
are collected in the object's \code{issues} element instead of failing the read.
\item \strong{Columns are named by accession, not by term name.} The PSI-MS names of
two accessions have changed over time, accessions have been merged, and
two terms can share a name; the accession cannot. Use \code{useAccession = FALSE} on a \verb{*Table()} accessor to get the term names instead, and
\code{\link[=cvParameter]{cvParameter()}} when writing code that has to survive a rename.
\item \strong{\code{spectraPeaks()}, not \code{peaksData()}.} The peak accessor is named after
what it returns rather than reusing the \code{peaksData()} generic of
ProtGenerics, so that this package has no dependency on the naming
decisions of the mass spectrometry infrastructure and a caller can use it
without attaching anything.
}
}

\section{JSON serialization}{

The two serializations describe the same model, and the object holds that
model, not the file. Where they correspond is stated in the specification;
where they do not, this package behaves as follows.\tabular{ll}{
   text serialization \tab JSON serialization \cr
   \verb{<mzSpecLib>} section \tab \code{attributes} \cr
   \verb{<AttributeSet Spectrum=name>} \tab \code{spectrum_attribute_sets} (and the three other scopes) \cr
   \verb{<Cluster=N>} \tab \code{clusters}, without its key (see below) \cr
   \verb{<Spectrum=N>} \tab \code{spectra} (no key: the identity is the \code{MS:1003237} attribute) \cr
   \verb{<Analyte=N>} / \verb{<Interpretation=M>} \tab \code{analytes} / \code{interpretations}, objects keyed by that number \cr
   \verb{<InterpretationMember=N>} \tab \code{member_interpretations} of the interpretation \cr
   `accession \tab name=value` with a group designator in brackets \cr
   `accession \tab name=ACC \cr
   columns 1 and 2 of a peak row \tab \code{mzs}, \code{intensities} \cr
   column 3 of a peak row \tab \code{peak_annotations}, \code{"?"} for an unannotated peak \cr
   columns 4+ of a peak row \tab \code{aggregations} \cr
}


Reading is permissive: a string, a list of strings or a list of structured
mzPAF annotation objects is accepted as the annotation of a peak. The first
two become the \code{annotation} column of the peak table; structured mzPAF
annotation \emph{objects} are kept as their JSON text, because this package does
not implement the mzPAF annotation grammar, and are written back as objects
by \code{\link[=write_mzspeclib]{write_mzspeclib()}} when the target is JSON.

Two deviations of the format itself are worth knowing, because both make a
strictly schema-validating reader fail on the specification's own examples.
The \code{spectrum} object of \code{mzSpecLib_json.schema.json} allows no
\code{interpretations} member, although the same schema defines the
\code{interpretation} object and although every spectrum of the example libraries
has interpretations; and the schema wants the per-peak annotations to be an
array of arrays, while the example libraries - and the writer of the
reference python implementation - write one string per peak. Both forms are
read. This package does not validate documents against the schema.

Two limitations of the \emph{writer} follow from the same schema and from the
decision to know no ontology, and both are stated rather than hidden:
\itemize{
\item A JSON document types its values, a text file does not, and in the
reference implementation it is the range a CV term declares in the
ontology - \code{xsd:float} for \verb{MS:1000045|collision energy}, \code{xsd:string} for
\verb{MS:1003186|library format version} - that decides whether a value is
written as a number or as a string. That range is not in the file: a text
library holds both as the text \code{"39.0"} and \code{"1.0"}, and no property of
those texts tells them apart. This package therefore types a value only
where typing is \emph{reversible}, i.e. where reading the document back yields
exactly the text that stood in the file, and keeps the text otherwise. A
value such as \code{"39.0"} consequently stays a string, and the validation
rules of the format report the term's declared numeric range as violated.
Typing every value that parses as a number instead would write the version
terms as \code{1} and \code{6} - which violates just as many rules of the same level
and destroys the value a version term holds.
\item A \verb{<Cluster=N>} key cannot be written to JSON at all: the \code{cluster} object
of the schema has \code{attributes} as its only property and allows no other,
and no CV term stands for a cluster key. \code{\link[=write_mzspeclib]{write_mzspeclib()}} warns about
it, because the key of the library's clusters is then not in the file it
writes. The attributes of the clusters survive unchanged.
}
}

\section{Round trips}{

Reading and writing a text library reproduces its metadata byte for byte;
peak numbers are re-rendered with \code{digits} significant digits, so they are
guaranteed numerically equal rather than identical. Reading and writing a
JSON library reproduces the metadata and the peak numbers as JSON numbers.
Three things cannot survive a change of serialization: the key of a
\verb{<Cluster>} section, which \code{\link[=write_mzspeclib]{write_mzspeclib()}} warns about, the \emph{order} of a
file that interleaved clusters and spectra, and comments (the specification
says comments are "generally NOT transcribed to other serializations"; the
reader counts them and keeps the count in the \code{comments} attribute of the
object it returns, so a caller can see that something was left out).
}

\section{The example file}{

\code{system.file("extdata", "example.mzSpecLib.txt", package = "mzspeclib")} is
a byte identical excerpt of \code{fetal_brain_tiny_consensus_td.mzSpecLib.txt}
from the \verb{examples/SpectraST/} directory of the HUPO-PSI mzSpecLib
specification repository, (c) HUPO-PSI, Apache License 2.0 - the library
header and the first three spectra, with nothing modified. It is the file the
examples of this manual page and of every accessor use. It exercises three
attribute sets, analytes, interpretations with member interpretations, mzPAF
peak annotations and peak rows with additional attribute columns; spectrum 3
is a consensus spectrum whose peak rows carry the three additional peak
attributes the header declares.

The file carries no comment lines, because section 4.1.1 requires a text
library to begin with its \verb{<mzSpecLib>} line - a provenance comment in front
of it makes the file invalid - so the attribution lives here instead.
}

